# Line-ending policy for artifacts whose BYTES are a contract.
#
# Why this file exists (found the hard way, 2026-08-18, on the meter):
# `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows checks the corpus out as CRLF while the
# committed blob is LF. Every Stage A schedule pins `corpus_sha256` in its
# provenance, and those hashes were generated against the Windows working-tree
# form -- so replay worked on Windows and failed on Linux with "corpus drift
# detected". The frozen workload was only reproducible on the platform that
# froze it, which defeats the point of freezing it.
#
# `-text` means git never translates line endings in either direction: the
# working tree gets the blob's bytes verbatim on every platform. For a file
# whose SHA-256 is a reproducibility contract, that is the only safe setting.

# The pinned prompt corpus -- hashed into every schedule's provenance.
corpus/*.jsonl -text

# Benchmark evidence: raw request logs and TTFT/TPOT sample sidecars promoted
# into benchmarks/evidence/. These are the durable record BASELINE.md cites,
# and they are read back by scripts/compute_point_metrics.py offline.
benchmarks/**/*.jsonl -text
